WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.033 --> 00:00:03.503
Welcome to the White Lotus
in Sicily.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:08.742
Let's do this!

00:00:09.943 --> 00:00:12.211
MIKE WHITE:<i> Last season
 really got into privilege,</i>

00:00:12.411 --> 00:00:14.314 line:0%
and this season's more of a

00:00:14.414 --> 00:00:16.683 line:0%
kind of bedroom farce
with teeth.

00:00:17.617 --> 00:00:18.518
(KNOCKING ON DOOR)

00:00:19.485 --> 00:00:20.787
<i> Buona sera.</i>

00:00:20.887 --> 00:00:23.523 line:0%
It's about how awkward
we all are,

00:00:23.623 --> 00:00:26.859
<i>and how sexual tension prevents
 us from knowing each other,</i>

00:00:26.959 --> 00:00:28.361
<i> and being able
 to accept each other.</i>

00:00:28.794 --> 00:00:31.230 line:0%
There's a plot strand
that's about toxic masculinity

00:00:31.331 --> 00:00:32.865
<i> and trying to break the cycle.</i>

00:00:33.399 --> 00:00:35.401
THEO JAMES:
<i>Everyone is functioning in a way</i>

00:00:35.501 --> 00:00:38.571 line:0%
to manipulate or undercut
another character.

00:00:39.272 --> 00:00:41.240
I'm not a fool.
Stop flirting with my wife.

00:00:41.708 --> 00:00:46.179
ADAM DIMARCO:<i> Some of the themes
are infidelity, family bonding,</i>

00:00:46.513 --> 00:00:47.880 line:0%
murder. (LAUGHS)

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:49.449
It's kind of got everything.

00:00:51.051 --> 00:00:54.421 line:0%
Season two is decidedly sexier
than season one.

00:00:56.656 --> 00:00:59.124
<i> Also, the environment
 lends itself</i>

00:00:59.225 --> 00:01:01.294
<i> to that romantic, summer vibe.</i>

00:01:01.394 --> 00:01:04.130
I think Sicily
is the perfect place

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:07.533
for romance and sexual politics.

00:01:08.367 --> 00:01:10.103
BEATRICE GRANNÓ:
<i> Taormina is so special.</i>

00:01:10.203 --> 00:01:11.570
<i> It's an out of time place,</i>

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:13.873 line:0%
and you feel like
you're in this bubble.

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:17.176
MIKE:<i> When we saw
 the Four Seasons
 in Taormina,</i>

00:01:17.276 --> 00:01:19.712
it was just such
a beautiful location,

00:01:19.812 --> 00:01:22.048
<i> and it just sold this
 kind of old world,</i>

00:01:22.248 --> 00:01:25.185
<i> European summer vacation.</i>

00:01:28.487 --> 00:01:30.289
<i> The first season,
 we were lucky to work with</i>

00:01:30.389 --> 00:01:32.291
<i> such an eclectic range
 of actors.</i>

00:01:32.691 --> 00:01:35.394
I really wanted to duplicate
that again this season.

00:01:35.494 --> 00:01:37.563
JON GRIES:<i> Any time I shoot
 with Jennifer Coolidge,</i>

00:01:37.663 --> 00:01:39.833 line:0%
the hardest part
is to keep my concentration

00:01:39.933 --> 00:01:40.933 line:0%
and not to laugh.

00:01:41.034 --> 00:01:42.769
(COUGHING)

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:45.672
(GROANS) I swallowed a bug.

00:01:46.406 --> 00:01:48.408
The great thing about season two

00:01:48.508 --> 00:01:51.945
is Tanya really gets to
come out of her shell.

00:01:52.378 --> 00:01:53.512
What if I take a look
in the mirror,

00:01:53.612 --> 00:01:55.314
and then just, like, bite my arm
really hard?

00:01:55.414 --> 00:01:57.717
-(LAUGHTER)
-Maybe not that.

00:01:58.918 --> 00:02:00.119
(YELLS PLAYFULLY)

00:02:00.219 --> 00:02:04.690 line:0%
Mike White. (LAUGHS)
He brings a positive energy.

00:02:05.057 --> 00:02:07.093 line:0%
To me, Mike, he's a maestro.

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:12.832
<i> He can make you laugh and cry
 about life in the same time.</i>

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:17.337
WILL SHARPE:
<i> I'm so impressed with how
his scripts spin so many plates.</i>

00:02:17.437 --> 00:02:20.206 line:0%
He takes care of
all the different characters
in the ensemble

00:02:20.306 --> 00:02:22.241
and weaves the stories together.

00:02:22.942 --> 00:02:25.044
HALEY LU RICHARDSON:
<i> We're all a protagonist
 to our own story,</i>

00:02:25.344 --> 00:02:26.345 line:0%
and antagonist to someone else.

00:02:27.312 --> 00:02:30.850
Mike can write characters
that don't have a lot
of redeeming qualities,

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:32.819
<i> and he gives them a break.</i>

00:02:34.853 --> 00:02:37.390
MICHAEL IMPERIOLI:
<i> It's a great company of people
 who like each other,</i>

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:38.491 line:0%
and like what they're doing,

00:02:38.591 --> 00:02:40.025 line:0%
and are proud
of what they're doing.

00:02:41.093 --> 00:02:44.797 line:0%
You bond on a set like this
because there's no way out.

00:02:45.198 --> 00:02:47.567
The true romance for me
of this production

00:02:47.667 --> 00:02:49.769
is just getting to have
this experience

00:02:49.869 --> 00:02:52.271
<i> with people that would end up
 becoming close friends</i>

00:02:52.371 --> 00:02:55.275
<i> and having this intense,
 collaborative experience.</i>

